WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:02.210
Alright, thanks for stopping by today.

00:00:02.210 --> 00:00:06.900
My name is Bradley Knapp, I'm a Product Manager
here at IBM, and I want to talk to you today

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:16.890
about how to use Intel Optane DC persistent memory, or PMEM, for SAP HANA. So, the actual HANA database itself.

00:00:17.119 --> 00:00:23.300
Intel and SAP cooperated very closely in the
development of PMEM and the development of

00:00:23.300 --> 00:00:29.179
HANA to allow you to put it on top of PMEM,
and the ability to do so was actually introduced

00:00:29.179 --> 00:00:34.630
in HANA 2.3 back a couple of years ago. And
so, before we get too far into that I did want

00:00:34.630 --> 00:00:40.190
to take just a second let's talk about what
PMEM is, what this Optane DC persistent memory

00:00:40.190 --> 00:00:47.420
product is. So, Optane DC persistent memory is
a NAND chip, right? So, it's a persistent chip

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:53.210
similar to what you would find on an Optane
SSD or an Optane PCIe drive, but it's in a

00:00:53.210 --> 00:00:58.489
DIMM form factor. So it's on the the actual
piece of silicon that goes into a DIMM slot

00:00:58.489 --> 00:01:01.150
just like RAM does. Why is this important?

00:01:01.150 --> 00:01:06.429
Well, if you think about our storage pyramid, right, where
we've got tape at the bottom, and then we've

00:01:06.429 --> 00:01:13.039
got hard disks, and then we've got normal SSDs
and PCIe drives - the higher the throughput

00:01:13.039 --> 00:01:17.579
and the lower the latency between the processor
and the storage, you're gonna see increased

00:01:17.579 --> 00:01:23.530
performance, right? Performance always goes
up, and so PMEM occupies an interesting spot

00:01:23.530 --> 00:01:31.259
between DRAM, which is the lowest latency but
the highest cost, and NVMe storage and PCIe drives.

00:01:31.259 --> 00:01:38.609
And so, by filling that gap where PMEM is orders
of magnitude faster than a normal SSD, but less

00:01:38.609 --> 00:01:40.200
expensive than RAM.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.340
It really allows you to do additional system
consolidation that wouldn't be possible if

00:01:44.340 --> 00:01:49.310
you were running on a system with just RAM. And
so, whenever you order a server that's got

00:01:49.310 --> 00:01:54.530
PMEM on it from IBM Cloud you're gonna receive
something that's set up, it's ready to go, it's

00:01:54.530 --> 00:02:00.684
fully provisioned, and it has everything you
need to go ahead and start installing SAP HANA on it.

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:05.650
And so, that means that the, that the PMEM is
already set in app direct mode, that it's already

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:11.780
had the namespaces applied, and so really you can
go in and start that HANA installation. Alright, and

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.631
so now that we're ready to actually install
that HANA database we need to think about a

00:02:15.631 --> 00:02:21.640
little bit about, “how do we do that installation?”
- and the answer is HANA takes care of all of

00:02:21.640 --> 00:02:25.890
it for you. Remember, the whole idea behind
HANA is that you've got a columnar database

00:02:25.890 --> 00:02:30.938
that's running in memory-and remember those
columns, that column data store, that's where

00:02:30.938 --> 00:02:36.873
95% of your data is - and that column store
is what we're going to put into the PMEM. 

00:02:36.873 --> 00:02:42.670
That's what's going to run in the persistent layer. The
volatile piece, the volatile data structures, which

00:02:42.670 --> 00:02:47.320
is the row store, that's going to continue
to run in RAM, just like it always has, because

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:52.530
you need that lower latency and you need that
a higher level of access, but remember that

00:02:52.530 --> 00:02:56.262
volatile piece, the row store, that's only 5%
of your data.

00:02:56.651 --> 00:03:01.150
And so, you're gonna put that 5% on the
RAM, the 95%, the column store, that goes onto

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:06.650
the PMEM, and then just like always your log
array, your data array, and your shared array, those go

00:03:06.650 --> 00:03:07.830
on traditional disk, right?

00:03:07.830 --> 00:03:12.870
You're still gonna write those down, but the
benefit of having all of this extra space

00:03:12.870 --> 00:03:20.659
available in the PMEM for your column store, means
that you can use technologies like MDC or MCOS to

00:03:20.659 --> 00:03:27.100
put additional HANA databases on to this same
physical system, moving all of those additional

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:32.571
databases onto the same system makes it possible
to decrease your total footprint size, 

00:03:32.571 --> 00:03:37.680
which is going to bring down your cost. You're actually
consolidating multiple physical machines onto

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.532
a single machine, and the benefits of that is
it's gonna dramatically decrease your 

00:03:42.532 --> 00:03:43.972
total cost of ownership.

00:03:44.361 --> 00:03:49.400
And then, in addition to the to the decreased
cost that you get by doing that consolidation, let's

00:03:49.400 --> 00:03:57.450
also not forget that the PMEM itself is persistent, right? The
data, the column store that's residing on it, it

00:03:57.450 --> 00:04:02.570
survives through a power cycle - so if you have
to do a system restart as part of your update

00:04:02.570 --> 00:04:07.090
cycle, or anything like that, you don't have
to go through that very painful process of

00:04:07.090 --> 00:04:12.760
having to reload all of your tables from the
data store back up into memory again.

00:04:12.760 --> 00:04:16.780
They're already there because they were running
on that persistent layer and got you through

00:04:16.780 --> 00:04:22.256
the power cycle. So, that's kind of an overview
of how you would put HANA on top of PMEM in

00:04:22.256 --> 00:04:23.419
App Direct mode. 

00:04:23.523 --> 00:04:26.815
